\label{def:displacement map}\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \ref{def:displacement map} def:displacement map}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{def:displacement map} def:displacement map}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{def:displacement map} def:displacement map}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{def:displacement map} def:displacement map}\ignorespaces\fi
The displacement map $\operatorname{disp}: \operatorname{VP} \rightarrow \operatorname{ED}$ is defined by 
\begin{equation*}
\operatorname{disp}(X)(e_i) := X(\operatorname{head} e_i) - X(\operatorname{tail} e_i).
\end{equation*}

We observe that in our bases, $\partial^*(X_{ik}) = \sum_{j=1}^\mathbf{e} \operatorname{ht}_{kj} W_{ij}$, where 
\begin{equation*}
\operatorname{ht}_{kj} = 
\begin{cases}
0, & \text{if $v_k = \operatorname{head}(e_j) = \operatorname{tail}(e_j)$}\\
+1, & \text{if $v_k = \operatorname{head}(e_j)$} \\
-1, & \text{if $v_k = \operatorname{tail}(e_j)$} \\
0, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
It follows that $\partial^*(X)(e_j) = X(\operatorname{head}(e_j) - \operatorname{tail}(e_j)) = \operatorname{disp}(X)(e_j)$.

\label{def:L}\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \ref{def:L} def:L}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{def:L} def:L}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{def:L} def:L}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{def:L} def:L}\ignorespaces\fi
The \emph{graph Laplacian} $L \colon \operatorname{VC} \rightarrow \operatorname{VC}$ is defined by $L = \partial \partial^T$. Thought of as a matrix with respect to the standard basis $v_1, \dotsc, v_\mathbf{v}$ for $\operatorname{VC}$, we have
\begin{equation}
L_{ij} = 
\begin{cases}
\deg(v_i) - 2 \# \text{(loop edges $v_i \rightarrow v_i$)}, & \text{if $i = j$,} \\
-\# \text{(edges $v_j \rightarrow v_i$)} - \# \text{(edges $v_i \rightarrow v_j$)}, 
& \text{if $i \neq j$}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\label{prop:basic Laplacian properties}\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:basic Laplacian properties} prop:basic Laplacian properties}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:basic Laplacian properties} prop:basic Laplacian properties}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:basic Laplacian properties} prop:basic Laplacian properties}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:basic Laplacian properties} prop:basic Laplacian properties}\ignorespaces\fi
The $\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}$ matrix $L$ is symmetric and positive semidefinite. We have
\begin{equation*}
\operatorname{im} L = \operatorname{im} \partial = \ker \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} \quad\text{and}\quad \ker L = \ker \partial^T = \operatorname{im} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}.
\end{equation*}

Since $L$ is symmetric, it is self-adjoint in the standard $\left< -, - \right>$ inner product on $\operatorname{VC}$ and, by Lemma~\ref{lem:orthogonal decomposition}, $\operatorname{VC} = \ker L \oplus \operatorname{im} L$ is an orthogonal decomposition of $\operatorname{VC}$. Using~Proposition~\ref{prop:basic Laplacian properties}, this means that any $x \in \operatorname{VC}$ can be written as $x = \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} y + \partial z$. Further,
\begin{equation*}
\tilde{L}x = (L + \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}})(\mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} y + \partial z) 
    	   = \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} y + L \partial z.
\end{equation*}
Since $L \partial z$ and $\mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} y$ are in the orthogonal subspaces $\operatorname{im} L$ and $\ker L$, we can first conclude that $\tilde{L}x = 0$ if and only if $x=0$. Further, if $x \in \operatorname{im} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}$, then $z = 0$, and $\tilde{L}x = \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} y \in \operatorname{im} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}$ while if $x \in \operatorname{im} \partial$, then $y = 0$ and $\tilde{L}x = L \partial z \in \operatorname{im} L = \operatorname{im} \partial$. Thus $\tilde{L}(\operatorname{im} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}) \subset \operatorname{im} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}$ and $\tilde{L}(\operatorname{im} \partial) \subset \operatorname{im} \partial$. Counting dimensions yields the reverse inclusions immediately.

Since the first two Moore--Penrose properties $F^+ F F^+ = F^+$ and $F F^+ F = F$ don't refer to an inner product, pseudoinverses computed with respect to any inner product obey these conditions.

To see that $\partial$ has the same Moore--Penrose pseudoinverse with respect to both the $\left< -, - \right>_{\tilde{L}^{-1}}$ and $\left< -, - \right>$ inner products on $\operatorname{VC}$, it suffices to check that if  $\partial^+ \partial$ and $\partial \partial^+$ are symmetric (that is, they are self-adjoint in $\left< -, - \right>$ on both $\operatorname{VC}$ and $\operatorname{EC}$), then $\partial \partial^+$ and $\partial^+ \partial$ are self-adjoint (in $\left( \operatorname{VC}, \left< -, - \right>_{\tilde{L}^{-1}} \right)$ and $\left( \operatorname{EC}, \left< -, - \right> \right)$). Since the inner product on $\left( \operatorname{EC}, \left< -, - \right> \right)$ is standard, $(\partial^+ \partial)^\dag = (\partial^+ \partial)^T = \partial^+ \partial$ immediately. So suppose we have computed $\partial^+$ with respect to $\left< -, - \right>$ on both $\operatorname{VC}$ and $\operatorname{EC}$. 
It's known~(\cite[eq.\ 7]{Ghosh2008}) that if $L^+$ is computed with respect to $\left< -, - \right>$ on $\operatorname{VC}$, then 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{L}^{-1} = L^+ + \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} = \partial^{T+} \partial^+ + \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}}\mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}.
\label{eq:Ltilde inverse}\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:Ltilde inverse} eq:Ltilde inverse}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:Ltilde inverse} eq:Ltilde inverse}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:Ltilde inverse} eq:Ltilde inverse}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:Ltilde inverse} eq:Ltilde inverse}\ignorespaces\fi
\end{equation}
Thus, using Lemma~\ref{lem:adjoint formula}, 
\begin{equation*}
(\partial \partial^+)^\dag = \tilde{L} (\partial \partial^+)^T \tilde{L}^{-1} = 
(L + \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}) \partial \partial^+ (L^+ + \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}). 
\end{equation*}
Proposition~\ref{prop:basic Laplacian properties} tells us that $\mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} \partial = 0$ since $\operatorname{im} \partial = \ker \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}$. Thus, we can simplify the right hand side above and get
\begin{equation*}
(\partial \partial^+)^\dag = 
L (\partial \partial^+)(L^+ + \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}) = 
L (\partial^{T+} \partial^T)(L^+ + \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}),
\label{eq:self-adjoint 2}\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:self-adjoint 2} eq:self-adjoint 2}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:self-adjoint 2} eq:self-adjoint 2}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:self-adjoint 2} eq:self-adjoint 2}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:self-adjoint 2} eq:self-adjoint 2}\ignorespaces\fi
\end{equation*}
where we used $\partial \partial^+ = (\partial \partial^+)^T = \partial^{T+} \partial^T$. Again,~Proposition~\ref{prop:basic Laplacian properties} tells us that $\partial^T \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} = 0$ since $\operatorname{im} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} = \ker \partial^T$. So we can simplify the right-hand side above and get
\begin{equation}
(\partial \partial^+)^\dag = 
L(\partial^{T+} \partial^T)L^+ = 
\partial (\partial^T \partial^{T+}) (\partial^T \partial^{T+}) \partial^+ = 
\partial \partial^+ \partial \partial^+ = \partial \partial^+ ,
\end{equation}
where we used $ \partial^{T} \partial^{T+} = (\partial^+ \partial)^T = \partial^+ \partial$ and $(\partial^+ \partial)(\partial^+ \partial) = \partial^+ \partial$. This proves that $\partial^+$ does not depend on whether we compute in $\left( \operatorname{VC}, \left< -, - \right>_{\tilde{L}^{-1}} \right)$ or $(\operatorname{VC},\left< -, - \right>)$.

To prove the second part, assume that $(\partial^T)^+$ has been computed with respect to $\left< -, - \right>$, so that $\partial^{T+} \partial^T$ and $\partial^T \partial^{T+}$ are symmetric. We must show that $\partial^{T+} \partial^T$ and $\partial^T \partial^{T+}$ are self-adjoint. But $\partial^{T+} \partial^T = (\partial \partial^+)^T = \partial \partial^+$, which we just proved is self-adjoint in $\left( \operatorname{VC}, \left< -, - \right>_{\tilde{L}^{-1}} \right)$, and $\partial^T \partial^{T+} = (\partial^+ \partial)^T = \partial^+ \partial$, which is symmetric and hence self-adjoint in $\left( \operatorname{EC}, \left< -, - \right> \right)$.

Last, if we assume that $L^+$ has been computed with respect to $\left< -, - \right>$, we know that $LL^+$ and $L^+L$ are symmetric and need to show they are self-adjoint. We note first that $L L^+ = \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{im} L}$ (in $\left< -, - \right>$) and $L^+ L = \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{im} L^T} = \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{im} L}$ since $L = L^T$. Thus $L L^+ = L^+ L$.
Now this proof goes exactly along the lines of the first one. As above,
\begin{equation*}
(L^+L)^\dag = \tilde{L}(L^+ L)^T\tilde{L}^{-1} = (L + \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}})(L L^{T+})\tilde{L}^{-1},
\end{equation*}
where we used $L^T = L$. But $\operatorname{im} L = \ker \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}$, so this simplifies to 
\begin{equation*}
(L^+ L)^\dag = L (L L^{T+}) \tilde{L} = L (L^+ L) (L^+ + \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}),
\end{equation*}
where we've used $L L^{T+} = L^T L^{T+} = (L^+ L)^T = L^+ L$. Since $\operatorname{im} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} = \ker L$, we have
\begin{equation*}
 (L^+ L)^\dag = L (L^+ L) (L^+ + \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}) = L L^+ L L^+ = L L^+ = L^+ L,
 \end{equation*}
 which completes the proof.

The proof is a computation. Using~Proposition~\ref{prop:samesies} and~\eqref{eq:Ltilde inverse} we have
\begin{align*}
\left< \partial u, \partial w \right>_{\tilde{L}^{-1}} = \left< \partial u, \tilde{L}^{-1} \partial w \right> = \left< \partial u, L^+ \partial w \right> + \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}}\left< \partial u, \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} \partial w \right>.
\end{align*}
On the far right $\left< \partial u, \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} \partial w \right> = \left< u, \partial^T \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} \partial w \right> = 0$,
since $\operatorname{im} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} = \ker \partial^T$ by~Proposition~\ref{prop:basic Laplacian properties}. Again using~Proposition~\ref{prop:samesies}, we are left with 
\begin{equation*}
\left< \partial u, \partial w \right>_{\tilde{L}^{-1}} = \left< \partial u, L^+ \partial w \right> = \left< \partial u, \partial^{T+} \partial^+ \partial w \right> = \left< \partial^+ \partial u, \partial^+ \partial w \right> = \left< \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{im} \partial^+} u, \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{im} \partial^+} w \right>.
\end{equation*}
Thus if $u, w$ are in the intial space $\operatorname{im} \partial^+$, we have $\left< \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{im} \partial^+} u, \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{im} \partial^+} w \right> = \left< u, w \right>$. Note that $\left< -, - \right>$ is our inner product on $\left( \operatorname{EC}, \left< -, - \right> \right)$, so we have completed the proof.

Suppose $X,Y \in \operatorname{VP}$. Now $\tilde{L} = \partial\partial^T + \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}}\mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}$, so $\tilde{L}^* = (\partial^{T*})(\partial^*) + \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}^*$ and
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:Bstar partial isometry}\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:Bstar partial isometry} eq:Bstar partial isometry}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:Bstar partial isometry} eq:Bstar partial isometry}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:Bstar partial isometry} eq:Bstar partial isometry}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:Bstar partial isometry} eq:Bstar partial isometry}\ignorespaces\fi
		\left< X, Y \right>_{\tilde{L}^*} = \left< X, \partial^{*T} \partial^* Y \right> + \left< X, \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}}\mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}^* Y \right> = \left< \partial^*X, \partial^*Y \right> + \left< X, \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}}\mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}^* Y \right>.
	\end{equation}	
Suppose $X, Y$ are in the initial space $\operatorname{im} (\partial^*)^\dag$. Since $\operatorname{im} (\partial^*)^\dag = \operatorname{im} (\partial^*)^T$,
we know $Y = (\partial^{*T})W$ for some $W \in \operatorname{ED}$. Then the second term in~\eqref{eq:Bstar partial isometry} is equal to
	\begin{equation*}
		\left< X, \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}}\mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}^* \partial^{T*} W \right>  = \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}} \left< X, (\partial^T \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}})^* W \right> = 0,
	\end{equation*}
	since $\operatorname{im} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}} =  \ker \partial^T$.

We know from~Proposition~\ref{prop:ker and im of bdystar} that $\dim \operatorname{im} \partial^* = d(\mathbf{v} - 1)$. Therefore \begin{equation*}
\dim \operatorname{ID} = \dim (\operatorname{im} \partial^*)^\perp = \dim \operatorname{ED} - \dim \operatorname{im} \partial^* = d(\mathbf{e} - \mathbf{v} + 1) = d \xi(\mathbf{G}).
\end{equation*}
We know that $\operatorname{ID} \oplus \operatorname{im} \partial^*$ is an orthogonal decomposition of $\operatorname{ED}$, so every $W \in \operatorname{ED}$ can be written as $W = W_{\operatorname{ID}} + W_{\operatorname{im} \partial^*}$. But $(\operatorname{im} \partial^*) = (\ker \partial)^0$, so $W(\ell) = W_{\operatorname{ID}}(\ell)$, as required. The last claim follows immediately from $(\operatorname{im} \partial^*) = (\ker \partial)^0$ as well.

\label{def:tjur conditional probability}\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \ref{def:tjur conditional probability} def:tjur conditional probability}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{def:tjur conditional probability} def:tjur conditional probability}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{def:tjur conditional probability} def:tjur conditional probability}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{def:tjur conditional probability} def:tjur conditional probability}\ignorespaces\fi
Suppose we have open sets $X \subset \mathbb{R}^m$ and $Y \subset \mathbb{R}^n$, a Radon probability measure $\mu$ on $X$, and a continuous map $t \colon X \rightarrow Y$. For any $t_\sharp \mu$-measurable set $B \subset Y$ with $(t_\sharp \mu)(B) > 0$, we can define a Radon probability measure $\mu^{B}$ on $X$ by
\begin{equation*}
\mu^B(f) = \frac{1}{(t_\sharp\mu)(B)} \int_{t^{-1}(B)} f(x) \mu(dx)
\end{equation*}
for any $f \in \mathcal{K}(X)$.\footnote{Remember, $\mathcal{K}(X)$ is the space of continuous functions on $X$ with compact support.}

For any $y \in Y$, a measure $\mu^y$ on $X$ is~\emph{the conditional distribution of $\mu$ given $t(x) = y$} if, for any $f \in \mathcal{K}(\mathbb{R}^m)$ and any $\epsilon > 0$, there is an open neighborhood $V$ of $y$ in $Y$ so that, for any $B \subset V$ with $t_\sharp \mu(B) > 0$, we have $\lvert\mu^y(f) - \mu^B(f)\rvert < \epsilon$. We note that if $\mu^y$ exists, it is unique. Further, it is concentrated on $t^{-1}(y)$.

\label{prop:disintegration with density}\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:disintegration with density} prop:disintegration with density}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:disintegration with density} prop:disintegration with density}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:disintegration with density} prop:disintegration with density}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:disintegration with density} prop:disintegration with density}\ignorespaces\fi
Suppose that $\mu$ on $\operatorname{ED}$ is $O(d)$-invariant and has a continuous density $p(Z)$ with respect to Lebesgue measure $\lambda^{d\mathbf{e}}$ on $\operatorname{ED}$. Further, suppose there are open sets $U' \in \operatorname{ED}$ and $U \in \operatorname{ID}$ so that $\operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{ID}}(U') = U$, $0 \in U$, and $p(Z) > 0$ on $U'$. Last, suppose $p(Z) \in o(\left\| Z \right\|^n)$ for all $n \in \mathbb{Z}$. 

Then $\mu$ is admissible and compatible with $\mathbf{G}$, $\mu_{\operatorname{ID}}$ has a continuous, positive density with respect to Lebesgue measure $\lambda^{d\xi(\mathbf{G})}$ on $U$ given by
\begin{equation*}
m_W = \int_{Z \in \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{ID}}^{-1}(W)} p(Z) \operatorname{\mathcal{H}}^{d(\mathbf{v}-1)}(dZ),
\end{equation*}
and the conditional distributions $\mu^W_{\mathbf{G}}$ (in the sense of~Definition~\ref{def:tjur conditional probability}) for all $W \in U$ are given explicitly by 
\begin{equation*}
\mu^W_{\mathbf{G}}(f) = \frac{1}{m_W} \int_{Z \in \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{ID}}^{-1}(W)} f(Z) p(Z) \operatorname{\mathcal{H}}^{d(\mathbf{v}-1)}(dZ).
\end{equation*}

As in the proof of~Proposition~\ref{prop:disintegration with density} we need only show that each 
\begin{equation*}
m_W = \int_{Z \in \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{ID}}^{-1}(W)} p(Z) \operatorname{\mathcal{H}}^{d(\mathbf{v} - 1)}(dZ) 
\end{equation*}
is positive and continuous in a neighborhood of $0$ in $\operatorname{ID}$. 

To see that $m_W$ is positive for small enough $W$, observe that $S(W) := \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{ID}}^{-1}(W)$ is an affine subspace of $\operatorname{ED}$ whose closest point is $\left\| W \right\|$ from $0$. Therefore, by choosing $W$ small enough, we can guarantee that $S(W)$ intersects any open neighborhood of $0$ in $\operatorname{ED}$ in a set of positive measure. 

Now we know that 
\begin{equation*}
p(Z) = p_1(\left\| Z(e_1) \right\|) \,\cdots\, p_\mathbf{e}(\left\| Z(e_\mathbf{e}) \right\|).
\end{equation*}
It's an exercise to show that 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ri bounds}\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:ri bounds} eq:ri bounds}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:ri bounds} eq:ri bounds}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:ri bounds} eq:ri bounds}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:ri bounds} eq:ri bounds}\ignorespaces\fi
\frac{1}{\sqrt{\mathbf{e}}} \left\| Z \right\| \leq \max_i \left\| Z(e_i) \right\| \leq \left\| Z \right\|.
\end{equation}
In particular, since we've assumed that each $p_i$ is positive in a neighborhood of $0$, there is some $B>0$ so that all $p_i(\left\| Z(e_i) \right\|)$ are positive for $\left\| Z(e_i) \right\| \leq \left\| Z \right\| \leq B$.
Thus for any $W$ with $\left\| W \right\| < B$, $p(Z)$ is positive on subset of $S(W)$ with positive measure. This establishes that these $m_W$ are positive.

Since the $p_i$ are all bounded, without loss of generality they are all bounded by a common $B>0$. It follows from~\eqref{eq:ri bounds} that for each $Z$ there is some $i$ so that
\begin{equation*}
p(Z) \leq B^{\mathbf{e}-1} p_i\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{\mathbf{e}}} \left\| Z \right\|\right). 
\end{equation*}
Since each $p_i(\left\| x \right\|)$ is in $o(\left\| x \right\|^{n})$ (and there are a fixed number of $p_i$), this implies that $p(Z)$ is in $o(\left\| Z \right\|^{n})$. The remainder of the argument follows as in the proof of~Proposition~\ref{prop:disintegration with density}.

\label{prop:conditional probability for arms}\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:conditional probability for arms} prop:conditional probability for arms}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:conditional probability for arms} prop:conditional probability for arms}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:conditional probability for arms} prop:conditional probability for arms}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:conditional probability for arms} prop:conditional probability for arms}\ignorespaces\fi
If $\mathbf{e} \geq 3$, $X = \mathbb{R}^{3\mathbf{e}}$, and $\mu$ is the product of uniform area measures $\mu_i$ on the unit spheres $S^2 \subset \mathbb{R}^3$, $Y = \mathbb{R}^3$, and $\operatorname{ftc} \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is the vector sum $\operatorname{ftc}(x) = x_1 + \cdots + x_\mathbf{e} = y$, then there are well-defined conditional probabilities $\mu^y$ for each $y$ with $\left\| y \right\| < \mathbf{e}$ and a measure 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:failure to close for equilateral edges}\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:failure to close for equilateral edges} eq:failure to close for equilateral edges}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:failure to close for equilateral edges} eq:failure to close for equilateral edges}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:failure to close for equilateral edges} eq:failure to close for equilateral edges}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{eq:failure to close for equilateral edges} eq:failure to close for equilateral edges}\ignorespaces\fi
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{ftc}_\sharp \mu(y) &= \left(\frac{1}{2\pi^2 \ell} \int_0^\infty s \sin \ell s \operatorname{sinc}^\mathbf{e} s \,d{s} \right) \lambda^{3}(dy) \\
 &= \left( \frac{\mathbf{e}-1}{2^{\mathbf{e}+1}\pi\ell}\sum_{k=0}^{\mathbf{e}-1}\frac{(-1)^k }{k!(\mathbf{e}-k-1)!}  \left((\mathbf{e}+\ell-2k-2)_+^{\mathbf{e}-2}-(\mathbf{e}+\ell-2k)_+^{\mathbf{e}-2}\right)  \right) \lambda^{3}(dy)
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $x_+ = \max \{ x, 0\}$ and $\ell = \left\| y \right\|$. These also form a decomposition of $\mu$ with respect to $\operatorname{ftc}$.

The computation of the pushforward density and the expression as a $\operatorname{sinc}$ integral can be traced back to Rayleigh~\cite{Rayleigh:1919do}. The existence of the conditional probabilities is more or less standard, but we outline the argument in order to connect it to decompositions explicitly.  

We start by defining a singular set $\Delta \subset (S^2)^\mathbf{e}$ as the set of all configurations where $x_i = \pm x_j$ for all $i, j \in 1, \dots, \mathbf{e}$. This is a finite union of submanifolds of dimension $2$ inside $(S^2)^\mathbf{e}$ and so we note that $\operatorname{\mathcal{H}}^{k}(\Delta) = 0$ for any $k > 2$. 

We now restrict our attention to $(S^2)^\mathbf{e} - \Delta$. The map $(z,\theta) \mapsto (\sqrt{1 - z^2} \cos \theta, \sqrt{1 - z^2} \sin \theta, z)$ writing $S^2$ in cylindrical coordinates is area-preserving and so pushes forward Lebesgue measure $\lambda^2(dz d\theta)$ to the area measure on $S^2$. These particular coordinates don't cover the north and south poles $(0,0,\pm 1)$, but employing similar constructions with respect to $x$ and $y$ and a partition of unity, we may construct a finite number of coordinate patches $\operatorname{cyl}_k \colon Z_k \rightarrow X_k$ where the $Z_k$ are an open cover of $(-1,1)^\mathbf{e} \times (0,2\pi)^\mathbf{e}$, the $X_k$ are an open cover of $(S^2)^\mathbf{e}$, the $\operatorname{cyl}_k$ are surjective, and each patch comes with a smooth, positive density function $\alpha_k$ so $\sum (\operatorname{cyl}_k)_\sharp \alpha_k \lambda^{2 \mathbf{e}} = \mu$. We define $\Delta_k := (\operatorname{cyl}_k)^{-1}(\Delta)$, and note that $\Delta_k$ is some dimension $2$ submanifold of $Z_k$.

We now consider the maps $\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k \colon Z_k \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^3$. Since $\operatorname{cyl}_k$ is a diffeomorphism, its differential is always invertible. But the differential of $\operatorname{ftc}$ at $x$ is not invertible if and only if all the $x_i$ are colinear: exactly when $x \in \Delta$. Combining these, we see that the differential of $\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k$ is surjective on $Z_k - \Delta_k$. The normal Jacobian $J_3(\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k) = \det( D\operatorname{ftc} D\operatorname{cyl}_k D\operatorname{cyl}_k^T D\operatorname{ftc}^T )^{1/2}$ is therefore positive on $Z_k - \Delta_k$.

By Theorem 8.1 of~\cite{Tjur1975}, we can construct decompositions $\alpha_k^y$ of each $\alpha_k  \lambda^{2\mathbf{e}}$ with respect to $\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k$ on the open sets $Z_k - \Delta_k$, where
\begin{equation*}
q_k(y) = \int\limits_{z \in (\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k)^{-1}(y) - \Delta_k} \frac{\alpha_k(z)}{J_3 (\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k)(z)} \operatorname{\mathcal{H}}^{2\mathbf{e}-3}(dz)
\end{equation*}
and\footnote{Here the notation $\nu \lefthalfcup U$ means the restriction of the measure $\nu$ to the subset $U$.} 
\begin{equation*}
\alpha_k^y = \frac{1}{q_k(y)} \frac{\alpha_k(z)}{J_3 (\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k)(z)} (\operatorname{\mathcal{H}}^{2\mathbf{e}-3} \lefthalfcup ((\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k)^{-1}(y) - \Delta_k))(dz)
\end{equation*}
and the pushforward measure $(\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k)_\sharp \alpha_k \lambda^{2\mathbf{e}}$ has density $q_k(y)$ with respect to $\lambda^{3}$ as long as the $q_k(y)$ are continuous and positive in $y$. The $q_k$ are integrals of positive quantities and hence positive; to see that they are finite recall that $\sum_k (\operatorname{cyl}_k)_\sharp \alpha_k \lambda^{2\mathbf{e}} = \mu$, so $\sum_k (\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k)_\# \alpha_k \lambda^{2\mathbf{e}} = \operatorname{ftc}_\# \mu$, which is given by the finite positive density in~\eqref{eq:failure to close for equilateral edges}.

We now want to assemble our work. We can define a map $\operatorname{cyl} \colon \bigsqcup_k (Z_k - \Delta_k) \rightarrow (S^2)^\mathbf{e} - \Delta$ by letting the restriction of $\operatorname{cyl}$ to $Z_k - \Delta_k$ be $\operatorname{cyl}_k$ and a measure $\alpha$ on $\bigsqcup_k (Z_k - \Delta)$ whose restriction to $Z_k - \Delta_k$ is $\alpha_k \lambda^{2\mathbf{e}}$. If we also define $\alpha^y$ on $\bigsqcup_k (Z_k - \Delta)$ by letting its restriction on $Z_k - \Delta$ be $\alpha_k^y$, it then follows that the $\alpha^y$ are a decomposition of $\alpha$ with respect to $\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}$. Now we have noted above that $\operatorname{\mathcal{H}}^{\alpha}(Z_k) = 0$ for any $\alpha > 2$. Since $\mathbf{e} \geq 3$, we see that $\operatorname{\mathcal{H}}^{2\mathbf{e}-3}(Z_k) = 0$ and we may rewrite our decomposition as 
\begin{equation*}
q_k(y) = \int\limits_{z \in (\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k)^{-1}(y)} \frac{\alpha_k(z)}{J_3 (\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k)(z)} \operatorname{\mathcal{H}}^{2\mathbf{e}-3}(dz)
\end{equation*}
and 
\begin{equation*}
\alpha_k^y = \frac{1}{q_k(y)} \frac{\alpha_k(z)}{J_3 (\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k)(z)} (\operatorname{\mathcal{H}}^{2\mathbf{e}-3} \lefthalfcup (\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl}_k)^{-1}(y))(dz)
\end{equation*}
without changing it, as long as (by convention) we replace the integrand by $1$ where it is not defined. Therefore the $\alpha^y$ are~\emph{also} a decomposition of $\alpha$ with respect to $\operatorname{ftc} \circ \operatorname{cyl} \colon \bigsqcup_k Z_k \rightarrow (S^2)^\mathbf{e}$. We would like to call attention to this step because it is the only one in the proof which is nonstandard-- in a generic situation, one would know from Sard's theorem that the singular set $Z$ had $\operatorname{\mathcal{H}}^{2\mathbf{e}}$-measure zero, so it could be ignored when computing expectations over all of $(S^2)^\mathbf{e}$ with respect to $\mu$, but one would~\emph{not} then be able to conclude that you could ignore the singular set when computing expectations with respect to~\emph{conditional} probabilities, which are integrals over lower-dimensional spaces.

We know that $\mu = \sum (\operatorname{cyl}_k)_\sharp \alpha_k \lambda^{2 \mathbf{e}} = \operatorname{cyl}_\sharp \alpha$. We define $\mu^y = \operatorname{cyl}_\sharp \alpha^y$. It follows from~Lemma~\ref{lem:decompositions push forward} that the $\mu^y$ are a decomposition of $\mu$ with respect to $\operatorname{ftc} \colon (\mathbb{R}^3)^\mathbf{e} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^3$.

\label{prop:centered}\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:centered} prop:centered}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:centered} prop:centered}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:centered} prop:centered}\ignorespaces\fi\ifinner\else\mgp{\raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \raggedright\tiny \ref{prop:centered} prop:centered}\ignorespaces\fi
The space $\operatorname{im} \partial^{*+}$ is the space of centered embeddings. We also have
\begin{equation*}
\{ \text{centered embeddings} \} = (\operatorname{im} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}})^0 = \ker \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v}}^* = (\ker \partial^T)^0 = \operatorname{im} \partial^{T*}
 = (\ker \partial^*)^\perp.
 \end{equation*}

Suppose we have a $d 	imes d$ matrix $Q \in O(d)$. The action of $Q$ on $\operatorname{VP}$ is multiplication by the $d\mathbf{v} 	imes d\mathbf{v}$ matrix $Q \otimes I_\mathbf{v}$. Since $
u$ is $O(d)$-invariant, we know $(Q \otimes I_\mathbf{v})_\sharp 
